---
layout: base.njk
# The title and description carry what people search for: an AI H5P generator, with ChatGPT and the
# like. The description counts the types as the hero does, in the release the page runs.
title: "Free AI H5P generator: make H5P with ChatGPT, Claude or Grok"
eleventyComputed:
  description: "Describe a lesson to ChatGPT, Claude or Grok and get an H5P activity: a quiz, a course presentation or any of {{ skill.offered.length }} H5P types. Free, no account, for Moodle."
# The picture a shared link shows: the page's first screen at 1200×630, light, with the sample
# settled. It is a screenshot, so take it again when that screen changes.
socialImage:
  src: /assets/og-h5p-build.png
  width: 1200
  height: 630
  alt: "The build page: its headline, Build an H5P activity with AI, and a course presentation playing beside it."
# The page's only policy: GitHub Pages sends no headers. base.njk writes it first in <head>, and the
# page's inline scripts, the site's color-scheme script and the one below, are added to script-src
# by their hashes at build (eleventy.config.js).
# Packages it built play in the player's frame, under the policy the player's Service Worker gives
# each response there. A file a source names by web address is fetched by the build, so any host may
# be connected to.
csp: "default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self'; img-src 'self' data: blob:; font-src 'self'; connect-src * data: blob:; worker-src 'self' blob:; frame-src 'self' blob:; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'self'"
---
{#- Two visitors, told apart by the address. One a chat sent, with the source in the link
    (#source=), gets the build at once. One who came with none gets a landing page: what it makes,
    a sample that plays, how to make their own. What is said to one alone is marked when-linked or
    when-unlinked, and site.css shows it by the class this line puts on <html> before the page
    paints; build-page.ts keeps the class in step with the address from then on. -#}
<script>if (new URLSearchParams(location.hash.slice(1)).has("source")) document.documentElement.classList.add("linked")</script>
<header class="topbar">
  <div class="wrap topbar-row">
    <a class="brand" href="/"><img src="/assets/logo.svg" alt="" width="24" height="24">{{ site.name }}</a>
    <div class="topbar-end">
      <nav class="topbar-links when-unlinked" aria-label="On this page">
        <a href="#make">Make your own</a>
        <a href="#build-it">Build</a>
        <a href="#cost">Build cost</a>
      </nav>
      {% include "theme-switch.njk" %}
    </div>
  </div>
</header>

{#- The markup build-page.ts wires up, built into /h5p/build/build-page.js by Vite (vite.config.ts). -#}
<main class="build" id="main">
  <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="page-title">
    <div class="wrap hero-grid">
      <div class="hero-words">
        <h1 id="page-title">Build an H5P activity with AI</h1>
        {#- The types are those the skill's table offers to choose from, in the release the page runs:
            counted from it (src/_data/skill.js), and linked at its tag. -#}
        <p class="lead when-unlinked">
          Describe a lesson to an AI chat, and get an interactive activity for your students: a
          quiz, flash cards, a branching scenario, an interactive book, or any of
          <a href="https://github.com/missing-elements/h5p-offline-player/blob/author-v{{ skill.version }}/skills/h5p-author/SKILL.md#1-choose-the-content-type-then-the-author-and-licence-with-the-user">{{ skill.offered.length }} H5P types</a>.
        </p>
        <div class="hero-actions when-unlinked">
          <a class="button button-solid button-large" href="#make">Make your own</a>
          <a class="button button-large" href="#build-it">Paste your chat's JSON</a>
        </div>
        <p class="lead when-linked">The link you opened carries an activity an AI chat wrote.</p>
        <p class="when-linked">
          This page builds it in your browser, where you play it and download a
          <code>.h5p</code> file that Moodle or any other H5P site takes. Nothing is uploaded.
        </p>
      </div>
      {#- The sample is /h5p/build/sample.h5p, which Vite builds from sample.json beside this file.
          build-page.ts gives the player its address only for a visitor who came with no source. -#}
      <figure class="sample when-unlinked" id="sample-figure">
        <div class="player-box">
          <p class="sample-note" id="sample-note" role="status"></p>
          <h5p-player id="sample"></h5p-player>
        </div>
        <figcaption>
          A sample made this way: a course presentation. Play it here, or
          <a href="/h5p/build/sample.h5p" download>download it</a> to try it in your Moodle or other
          LMS, on H5P.com or in Lumi.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band features when-unlinked" aria-labelledby="features-heading">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 id="features-heading" class="visually-hidden">What you get</h2>
      <ul class="feature-grid" role="list">
        <li class="feature">
          <span class="feature-icon icon-types" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <h3>Many kinds of activity</h3>
          <p>Quizzes, flash cards, fill in the blanks, drag the words, a branching scenario, an interactive book, a presentation and more.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="feature">
          <span class="feature-icon icon-ai" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <h3>Works with your AI chat</h3>
          <p>Claude, ChatGPT and Grok all work. A model you run on your own computer should work too, if it can open a web page.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="feature">
          <span class="feature-icon icon-browser" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <h3>Built in your browser</h3>
          <p>The page builds and plays the activity on your computer, free and with no account. Nothing you make is sent to this site.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="feature">
          <span class="feature-icon icon-school" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <h3>Ready for Moodle</h3>
          <p>The <code>.h5p</code> file carries the libraries an H5P site asks for, so Moodle or any other H5P site takes it.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="feature">
          <span class="feature-icon icon-chat" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          <h3>Mistakes go back to the chat</h3>
          <p>If the chat got something wrong, the page lists every problem, and one button copies them back for it to fix.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  {#- The prompt is a code block, which site.js gives a Copy button. It carries the skill's page at its
      whole address, which a chat's fetch tool needs. It names the content type, so the chat asks
      only for the author and licence: the sample is what Haiku wrote for it, with the site's name
      and licence put on it. -#}
  <section class="band band-tint make when-unlinked" id="make" aria-labelledby="make-heading">
    <div class="wrap">
      <header class="band-head">
        <h2 id="make-heading">Make your own</h2>
        <p>Three steps, with an AI chat that can open a web page.</p>
      </header>
      <ol class="how" role="list">
        <li>
          <h3>Ask your chat</h3>
          <p>Paste the prompt below into the chat, with a subject of your own.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Answer a question</h3>
          <p>The chat asks whose name and which licence go on it, which you may skip, and which kind of activity you want if the prompt names none. Then it writes the activity.</p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <h3>Play it, then download it</h3>
          <p>It hands over a link that opens the activity here, or its source, a block of JSON to paste under <a href="#build-it">Build it</a>. Some chats build the <code>.h5p</code> file themselves.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <div class="prompt-box">
        <pre class="language-text prompt" data-label="Prompt">Read {{ site.url }}/h5p/skill/ and follow it to make an H5P Course Presentation of six slides on writing learning objectives with Bloom's taxonomy, with a question on each slide.</pre>
        <p class="hint">
          The address in the prompt is <a href="/h5p/skill/">the h5p-author skill's page</a>: the
          instructions that tell a chat how to write an H5P activity, as text anyone can read.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {#- The steps are drawn as a timeline by site.css: a marker for each, on a rail that runs on to
      the next. It reads how a step stands from what the step says of itself, so the markers need no
      script: #source-state, the files in #file-list and #status, each by its data attribute. -#}
  <section class="band tool" id="build-it" aria-labelledby="build-it-heading">
    <div class="wrap">
      <header class="band-head">
        <h2 id="build-it-heading">Build it</h2>
        <p class="when-unlinked">Paste the JSON your chat wrote, build it, play it, and download the <code>.h5p</code> file.</p>
      </header>

      <section class="step" aria-labelledby="source-heading">
        <h3 id="source-heading"><span class="step-marker"><span class="step-number">1</span></span> Source</h3>
        <p class="when-unlinked">The source is your activity as the chat wrote it. Paste it here, or open it from a file.</p>
        <p class="when-linked">The source is your activity as the chat wrote it. The link carried it here.</p>
        <div class="field">
          <label for="source" id="source-label">The source, as JSON</label>
          <textarea id="source" rows="12" spellcheck="false" autocomplete="off" placeholder="The source your chat wrote goes here. It starts with { and ends with }."></textarea>
        </div>
        <p class="hint" id="source-state" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
        <p class="hint">
          You can change the source here before you build it. A source you paste whole or open is
          laid out for you. A mistake in the JSON is marked as you type, and each problem the build
          finds is marked where it is: choose its name in the list to go there.
          <span class="when-linked">This page's address follows your changes, so you can copy it to
          send the source on.</span>
        </p>
        <p>
          <label for="source-file" class="inline-label">Or open a file</label>
          <input type="file" id="source-file" accept=".json,application/json,text/plain">
        </p>
        <p class="when-unlinked">
          No source yet?
          <button type="button" id="sample-source" class="link-button">Use the sample's</button>
          <span class="hint" id="sample-source-result" role="status"></span>
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="step" id="files-step" aria-labelledby="files-heading" hidden>
        <h3 id="files-heading"><span class="step-marker"><span class="step-number">2</span></span> Files</h3>
        <p>The source names these files. Choose them from your computer; each is matched by its name.</p>
        {#- The lists are drawn without bullets, and Safari then reads one as a list only if it is told. -#}
        <ul class="file-list" id="file-list" role="list"></ul>
        <p>
          <label for="files" class="inline-label">Choose files</label>
          <input type="file" id="files" multiple>
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="step" aria-labelledby="build-heading">
        <h3 id="build-heading"><span class="step-marker"><span class="step-number" id="build-number">2</span></span> Build</h3>
        <p><button type="button" id="build" class="button button-solid button-large">Build</button></p>
        <p class="status" id="status" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
        <div id="problems-box" hidden>
          <ul class="messages problems" id="problems" role="list"></ul>
          <p>
            <button type="button" id="copy-problems" class="button button-large">Copy the problems for the chat</button>
            <span class="hint" id="copy-result" role="status"></span>
          </p>
          <p class="hint">Paste them into the chat that wrote the source: it fixes them all and writes the source again.</p>
          <p class="hint" id="copy-types" hidden></p>
        </div>
        <div id="notes-box" hidden>
          <h4>Notes</h4>
          <ul class="messages" id="notes" role="list"></ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="step" id="play-step" aria-labelledby="play-heading" hidden>
        <h3 id="play-heading"><span class="step-marker"><span class="step-number" id="play-number">3</span></span> Play and download</h3>
        <p>
          Work through all of it, wrong answers included, and check the answers and feedback against
          your material. The build checks the structure, not whether the content is right.
        </p>
        <p><a class="button button-solid button-large" id="download" download>Download the .h5p</a></p>
        <div class="player-box">
          <h5p-player id="player"></h5p-player>
        </div>
        <p class="hint">
          The package carries H5P's libraries, each under its own licence, most MIT, listed in
          <a href="/h5p/assets/libraries-LICENSES.txt">the pack's licence list</a>: check those terms
          before you share it.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </section>

  {#- What a build saves the chat that writes the source, in tokens. The figures are the sample's,
      counted at the site's build by src/_data/buildCost.js. -#}
  <section class="band band-tint cost when-unlinked" id="cost" aria-labelledby="cost-heading">
    <div class="wrap">
      <header class="band-head">
        <h2 id="cost-heading">Build cost, in tokens</h2>
        <p>
          An AI chat counts its work in tokens, about four characters each. Asked plainly for an H5P
          activity, a chat writes it the way H5P stores it, <code>content.json</code> and
          <code>h5p.json</code>, with the settings and button texts H5P's editor saves. With the
          h5p-author skill it writes only the source, what only an author can know, and the build
          writes the rest.
        </p>
      </header>
      <dl class="stats">
        <div class="stat">
          <dt>tokens the chat writes with the skill: the source, {{ buildCost.written.charactersText }} characters</dt>
          <dd>≈ {{ buildCost.written.tokensText }}</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="stat">
          <dt>tokens it writes when asked plainly: <code>content.json</code> and <code>h5p.json</code>, {{ buildCost.file.charactersText }} characters</dt>
          <dd>≈ {{ buildCost.file.tokensText }}</dd>
        </div>
        <div class="stat stat-accent">
          <dt>of it written by the build, not the chat</dt>
          <dd>{{ buildCost.saved.share }}%</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      <p class="stats-note">The sample at the top of this page, as JSON on one line, counted when this page was built.</p>
      <p class="cost-more">
        Reading costs too: to write a content type by hand, a chat first reads how H5P builds it,
        3,900 to 9,500 tokens for one type, where the skill's notes on a type are 200 to 3,000. For a
        five-question quiz the source was 1,000 to 1,800 tokens, and the content written by hand
        about 3,470
        (<a href="https://github.com/missing-elements/h5p-offline-player/blob/main/design/author.md">measured</a>).
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {#- Where else a package plays, with no H5P site, and where to say how it went: by email first, which
      asks for no account, or a public discussion for someone with a GitHub account. -#}
  <div class="band closing">
    <div class="wrap closing-grid">
      <section class="plays" aria-labelledby="plays-heading">
        <h2 id="plays-heading">Play it without an H5P site</h2>
        <p>
          Any H5P site takes the file you download. With none, use h5p-offline-player, the player
          this page plays it in:
          <a href="https://github.com/missing-elements/h5p-offline-player/blob/main/docs/setup.md">on a site of your own</a>,
          or <a href="https://h5p-offline-player.vercel.app/app/">offline, in its app</a>.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section class="feedback" aria-labelledby="feedback-heading">
        <h2 id="feedback-heading">How did it go?</h2>
        <p>
          Say what you made, and what went well or wrong with the chat's source or this page. Write
          to the author or, with a GitHub account, start a public discussion where the h5p-author
          skill is made.
        </p>
        <div class="feedback-actions">
          <a class="button button-primary button-large" href="{{ site.buildFeedback.email }}">Email feedback</a>
          <a class="button button-large" href="{{ site.buildFeedback.github }}">Discuss on GitHub</a>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>
  </div>
</main>

<footer class="site-foot">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p>
      Built with <a href="https://github.com/missing-elements/h5p-offline-player">h5p-offline-player</a>
      and <code>@missing-elements/h5p-author</code>, MIT. The H5P runtime the player loads is
      <a href="/h5p/assets/runtime-LICENSE.txt">GPL-3.0</a>. Not affiliated with H5P Group; H5P is a
      registered trademark of H5P Group AS.
    </p>
    <p>Found a mistake? <a href="https://github.com/missing-elements/missing-elements.github.io/blob/main/{{ page.inputPath | replace('./', '') }}">Edit this page</a> or <a href="https://github.com/missing-elements/h5p-offline-player/issues">open an issue</a>.</p>
  </div>
</footer>
{#- What the page is, for a search engine: a free web application for teaching, run in the browser.
    It is data, not a script that runs; the policy's hash for it (eleventy.config.js) is unused. -#}
<script type="application/ld+json">
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "WebApplication",
  "name": "AI H5P generator",
  "alternateName": "Build an H5P activity with AI",
  "url": "{{ site.url }}{{ page.url }}",
  "description": {{ description | dump | safe }},
  "applicationCategory": "EducationalApplication",
  "operatingSystem": "Any",
  "browserRequirements": "Requires JavaScript",
  "isAccessibleForFree": true,
  "offers": { "@type": "Offer", "price": "0", "priceCurrency": "USD" },
  "publisher": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "{{ site.name }}", "url": "{{ site.url }}" }
}
</script>
<script type="module" src="/h5p/build/build-page.js"></script>
